THE CASE AGAINST THE TARGET:
SRC_003 violates the polynomial-cost guarantee it claims to preserve. Its gating predicate requires detecting 'a stable suspension pattern across two genuinely different school families' before auto-registering a probe warrant. Determining cross-family stability entails comparing school-family structure across iteration cycles (the 'stable ... pattern' language implies observing the boundary atlas across at least two runs or two family partitions). This is a meta-level analysis layered on top of the fixpoint computation, and its cost is not bounded by any polynomial in the argument graph size — school-family partitioning and cross-iteration stability checking can grow with the number of distinct family labels and iteration depth, which are not graph-polynomial terms. The proposal asserts 'polynomial cost is preserved because the fixpoint runs on the new edge set exactly as before,' but that only covers the downstream fixpoint run; it silently omits the cost of the upstream gating analysis that decides whether to synthesize the edge. A proposal cannot claim to preserve a guarantee while leaving the cost of its own trigger mechanism unaccounted.

TARGET:
Close the loop from suspension to edge synthesis: when the boundary atlas shows a stable suspension pattern across two genuinely different school families, auto-register a probe warrant that attacks the suspended cluster from an exogenous anchor — the LAMBDA_FLOOR role the spec calls load-bearing, not decorative. Because the new edge is a registered warrant with normal carriage rules, it faces the same scrutiny and can itself be attacked; per-run determinism and polynomial cost are preserved because the fixpoint runs on the new edge set exactly as before, and committed-root validity holds since new edges only ever attack, never invent acceptance. Counterexample: auto-generated warrants can be spurious, so synthesis must be gated on cross-family stability and the generated warrant must carry provenance back to the atlas that triggered it.